{
  "format": "kingmaker",
  "version": 2,
  "kind": "scene",
  "id": "drowned-rat",
  "title": "The Drowned Rat",
  "description": "A dockside dive with house rules, a landlady who sells gossip by the measure, and a big frightened man with a bandaged hand.",
  "lore": [
    "Places/The Drowned Rat.md",
    "World/Caerwyn now.md"
  ],
  "time": "evening",
  "place": "drowned-rat",
  "hook": "This morning a palace clerk, Wendell Prout, was found hanged from the sack hoist behind the King's Granary, and you learned there that a huge man with a bandaged right hand was at the granary with a gentleman the night he died: Big Hobb Mallory, champion of the arm-wrestling at the Drowned Rat on the river stairs. At the noon break Sergeant Kell, the watch sergeant the King lent you, saw a gentleman with a silver-topped cane walking beside the Purveyor's wagons, and at his elbow a big dock-man nursing a bandaged hand, who called him 'Master'. Prout went up on a hoist rope, and Kell reckons a rope takes the skin off the palm of whoever hauls on it. She followed the big man down the river stairs to the Drowned Rat. It's evening on the eve of the Assembly, and she has come back down with you, in a borrowed coat over her uniform that fools nobody.",
  "setting": "Evening on the eve of the Centennial Assembly, halfway down the river stairs at Caerwyn's docks. Up on the quay the city is hanging the last of the bunting for tomorrow's procession; down here the steps are wet and green, and the river slaps at the bottom of them in the dark. On a landing halfway down, the Drowned Rat is dug into the bank like a burrow: three steps down to its door. Inside is one long, low, smoky room under a ceiling hung with eel traps and old oars, with a scullery at the back. A driftwood fire burns blue with salt in the corner. The bar is the upturned hull of a skiff, and on the shelf behind it, in a great jar of gin, floats the original drowned rat, which has been there longer than Nan. Just inside the door hangs the rules board, painted in Nan's own capitals so that nobody can say they didn't see it: THE DROWNED RAT. RULES OF THE HOUSE. 1. No credit. 2. No blades out. 3. No fighting indoors. 4. No watch on duty. 5. No singing before the ninth bell. 6. What's said in the Rat stays in the Rat. 7. Gossip sold by the measure. By order, N. Quill. Enforced by Manners. (Nan sees no contradiction between six and seven: the gossip is sold in the Rat.) Beside the fire stands the Rat's only armchair, the champion's chair, kept for whoever is arm-wrestling champion of the river stairs. Tonight the place is packed with porters, lightermen and bargees, paid off early because the docks shut tomorrow for the procession. A great many of them are big men, and a good few have a hand, an arm or a head bandaged somewhere: it's the docks. Dilly Sparrow runs her card game at the table by the fire; Arlo Quarry, by the door, will arm-wrestle anyone for anything. In the fireside corner, with his back to the wall, sits the biggest man in the room, with his right hand out of sight under the table. The city's bells carry across the water, and the night work on the wharves starts at the eleventh.",
  "premise": "Can the player find the big man with the bandaged hand and get out of him what he knows (who he works for, what happened in the loft, the Heron warehouse and how to get in), and what becomes of Big Hobb when they do?",
  "truth": "Big Hobb Mallory, dock-porter and the Rat's arm-wrestling champion these eleven years, has been Master Josiah Sallow's muscle for a month, since the Heron warehouse began re-sacking the King's 'lost' grain by night: he works the Heron's hoist on loading nights for double pay in heron pennies, and does the Master's heavy work. Last night, a little after the midnight bells, Sallow took him to the granary tally loft 'to have a word with a clerk'. When Prout wouldn't give up the page he'd razored from the Roll, Sallow struck him with the head of his cane and strangled him with a cord while Hobb held his arms, then had Hobb haul him up on the sack hoist 'so it looks like he did it himself'. The rope took the skin off Hobb's right palm in a stripe straight across; he tied it off with a docker's knot. At first light they went back to look, and Sallow paid a granary girl (Moll) a silver crown to say she'd seen the clerk jump; Hobb called him 'Master Sallow' in front of her. At noon Hobb walked at the Master's elbow beside the Purveyor's flour wagons, nursing his hand, where Kell saw them, until the Master sent him off to drink until the night's work; Kell followed him down the river stairs to the Rat. Since the loft he's been drinking for two and losing his heron silver at Dilly's table on purpose. The Master has told him how patient the river is with men who talk, and knows where Hobb's mum lives, over a tripe-dresser's in the Shambles. Tonight's a loading night: Hobb must be on the Heron's hoist at the eleventh bell (in at the river door off the plank walk, showing the brass heron token on the string round his neck at its grille), the Master comes at the midnight bell to see the Saltmere barge Patience off, and she sails on the ebb at the second bell after midnight. Hobb doesn't know that Cob was in the loft, that Arlo saw his palm at the pump this evening, that Nan has put his hand and the hanged clerk together, that Kell followed him here, or that a man who swears for the Crown can sometimes escape the rope. In the room: Nan knows the gentleman is Master Sallow and won't say it in her house; Dilly knows he's due 'at the Heron' at the eleventh bell and guesses wrong (smuggling); Arlo saw the rope burn and is saving it for the third refusal; Cob, Nan's pot-boy tonight, knows the big cove from the loft by his size and his 'Yes, Master', has kept to the scullery since Hobb came in, and pinched one of his heron pennies off the pot tray. If the granary didn't turn it up, Prout's page is still sewn into the lining of the left boot on Cob's feet. Sallow never comes down the river stairs, and the Crake brothers are barred from the Rat.",
  "clock": "The eleventh bell, when the night work on the wharves starts. At the tenth bell Hobb counts his last heron pennies onto the table and says he's due at work; at the eleventh he gets up and goes, as he must, whatever is happening, because if he isn't at the river door when his master comes, his master will know. There's time to work on him and on the room, but the bell won't wait, and it's felt coming, a step nearer every few exchanges and never the same sign twice: the night men drinking up and reaching for their coats, the tenth bell across the water, Hobb's pile of heron pennies going down and his eyes on the door more often.",
  "limits": [
    "Seeing the King, or getting a warrant or men from the palace tonight: on the eve of the Assembly the palace is shut to everyone but the procession rehearsal and the delegations' dinner, and the Captain files what the Purveyor's office asks him to file. The gate guard will take a written message for the Marshal, and the King will see the investigator in the morning. Tonight it's the player and Kell.",
    "More watch (constables, the river watch, anyone to hold the stairs): the whole watch is lining the Assembly route through the night, the river watch with them. Kell is the watch here, off her patch.",
    "Credit, IOUs or 'the King will pay': rule one. Money on the bar, goods Nan will take at her own valuation, or nothing.",
    "Master Sallow himself, or anyone who'll say his name aloud for money: he wouldn't be seen dead on the river stairs, nobody in the Rat will say where he is, and Nan doesn't sell quarts.",
    "Another way out of the Rat, or a private room to talk in: it's one long room and a scullery. The only door opens onto the stairs; the scullery's slop hatch onto the river is just big enough for Cob, and the river under it is fast and cold.",
    "The player going anywhere else themselves before the bell (Hobb's lodgings, the palace offices, the watch house for help): the trail is here, and at the bell it goes downriver with Hobb. His lodging is a room in the Shambles across the city, with his mum in it. Sending is another matter, and not this: a written message to the Marshal by the palace gate, or someone to fetch Hobb's mum tonight and take her somewhere safe, if someone here will.",
    "Buying magic here (truth potions, charms, a hedge-wizard for hire): there's none to be had on the river stairs, and Nan sells gossip, not truth. (The player's own spells are theirs, and work as the rules say.)",
    "A pardon for Hobb, or the Crown's mercy promised tonight: a man who'd hang alongside the guilty can sometimes swear for the Crown against them, but that's the King's to grant, at the Assizes after the Assembly. Kell can't promise it and won't pretend to; she can write down what he says and speak for him. The player can promise only what's theirs to give."
  ],
  "known": [
    "Nan enforces the rules on the board by the door to the letter: one warning for small things (a raised voice, a threat, a song before the ninth bell) and none for a fist or a blade; after that it's Manners and the door, and you're barred until she says otherwise. Nobody is stopped at her door: outside it is the river's business.",
    "Nan's gossip is sold by the measure, money on the bar first: a gill (a penny) for what everyone on the stairs knows, a half (tuppence) for what most don't, a pint (fourpence) for what only Nan knows. She doesn't sell quarts: a quart'll get you drowned.",
    "The house custom at the arm-wrestling: elbows on the table, right hands, Nan referees, wagers paid at the bar. Anyone may challenge the champion, and a champion who refuses three challenges in one night gives up the chair: the Rat's only armchair, by the fire.",
    "Big Hobb Mallory has been champion of the Rat at the arm-wrestling eleven years and never beaten. He sits in the fireside corner every night, is polite to a fault, and sends his wages to his mum in the Shambles every week, without fail.",
    "Twenty years ago Nan put a constable of the watch out through the door (through it, not the doorway), and the watch still owes her four shillings and sixpence for it. The stairs still tell it, and the constable was Agnes Kell.",
    "Cob, a granary boy, does Nan's pots on busy nights for a penny and a bowl of eel stew; Nan barred his da, Nat Pike, for hitting him. Dilly Sparrow deals Tuppenny Bargee by the fire at tuppence a hand, and her barge, the Dabchick, is tied up at the foot of the stairs.",
    "The docks shut tomorrow for the procession, so the river paid off early and the Rat is packed. The night shift goes down at the eleventh bell, and the ebb runs at the second bell after midnight. The river watch, whose patch the stairs are, is up on the Assembly route with the rest of the watch.",
    "On the stairs, Saltmere barges have been loading by night all month at the Heron, the last warehouse at the downriver end of the wharves, and going downriver low in the water while bread goes up. New silver pennies with a heron where the King's face should be, the Heron's pay for night work, have been crossing the bar; the chandlers want the King's face, so Nan keeps them in a jar marked FOREIGN."
  ],
  "cast": [
    {
      "id": "hobb",
      "name": "Big Hobb Mallory",
      "called": "the big man with the bandaged hand",
      "lore": "Cast/Big Hobb Mallory/public.md",
      "look": "A dock-porter the size of a door: a head like a bollard, a nose broken more than once, shoulders that fill the corner, a leather porter's hood pushed back. His right hand is wrapped in a tarry rag, and he keeps it under the table. A greasy string round his thick neck disappears into his shirt, and every time the door opens, he flinches.",
      "voice": "Slow, low and polite: 'sir', 'missus', 'Mrs Quill', and 'Master', which he can't stop saying and stops himself after. Says as little as he can; when it spills, what spills is how he feels (the clerk's stockinged feet, the rope, his mum), not how anything works. Frightened, he goes very quiet and very still, like a dray-horse in thunder. 'Not tonight, Arlo.'",
      "wants": "Tonight: to lose every heron penny he has at Dilly's table, because he can't bear to keep them, and to drink until he stops seeing the clerk's stockinged feet; and still to be at the river door at the eleventh bell, so his master never looks at him twice. Underneath, to put it right, if anyone could show him a way that isn't the noose or the river.",
      "fears": "His master, who has told him how patient the river is with men who talk, and who knows where his mum lives. The noose, and the watch who'd put him in it. His hand being seen. His mum finding out what he did.",
      "whyHere": "He's in the fireside corner every night, and harder at it since the loft: drinking for two and losing his heron silver at Dilly's table because he can't bear to keep it, until the eleventh bell, when he has to be at the Heron's river door so his master never looks at him twice.",
      "knows": [
        "He's Hobb Mallory, porter, champion of the Rat at the arm-wrestling these eleven years. He won't wrestle tonight and won't say why.",
        "He has a night job for a gentleman that pays double, in new silver. He'll own to that much, and not to what it is.",
        "He's due at work at the eleventh bell, and if he isn't there when his master comes, his master will know something's wrong."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Shows: he won't wrestle tonight; his right hand stays under the table; he flinches at the door, and says 'Master' and stops himself. Torn: telling hangs him, and not telling leaves him the Master's for good. Opens, with no dice: a way to put it right that isn't the noose (he doesn't know a man can swear for the Crown), someone who'll see his mum safe whatever happens to him, or a kindness he can't stand up to. A fright worse than the Master works too, on a roll, and he never forgets who frightened it out of him. First: 'I only held the rope.'",
        "The loft, haltingly, and not where the whole Rat can hear: last night after the midnight bells he held a clerk's arms in the granary tally loft while the clerk was struck and strangled, then hauled him up on the sack hoist 'so it looks like he did it himself'. The rope took the skin off his palm. 'I only held him. I only held the rope.' He has been sick twice since.",
        "Who, which costs him most: he says 'the Master', and gives the name only when asked for it straight, in a whisper: Master Sallow of the Royal Purveyor's office, who struck the clerk with the head of his cane and used the cord.",
        "Only if asked: at first light the Master went back to look, and paid a granary girl a silver crown to say she'd seen the clerk jump.",
        "His night work, if asked: the Heron warehouse, at the downriver end of the wharves, re-sacking ('It's the King's grain, sir. Everyone knows. Nobody says.'). Tonight's a loading night, a barge for Saltmere; he's due on the hoist at the eleventh bell, and the Master comes at the midnight bell to see it off.",
        "How he gets in, if asked: a brass heron token on the string round his neck, shown at the grille of the river door off the plank walk. It's only brass, and the gang know his face at the grille, so he has no need of it tonight: he'll give it up if asked. He knows his own part of the night (the hoist, the door, the bell) and not how the rest of it works."
      ],
      "attitude": "Polite, slow and closed: he wants to be left alone with his drink. More frightened of his master than of anyone in this room, until someone gives him reason to be otherwise. Torn: the strongest man on the river stairs, and the most frightened man in the Rat. Threats he can stand (he's been threatened all his life); kindness undoes him, because it makes him want to tell. Push him clumsily and he bolts, and frightened men run to their masters. Arlo's challenge presses it: wrestle, and the hand shows; refuse a third time, and he gives up the chair in front of everyone. No fighter unless cornered, and then a door falling on you. Ties here: Mrs Quill, whose barrels he's carried since he was Cob's size; Dilly, who feeds him and is winning his silver; Arlo, who could have the chair and welcome, if it didn't mean showing the hand; Nan's loud little pot-boy, whom he once gave the end of his pie (he doesn't know the boy was in the loft).",
      "limits": "He never starts a fight; cornered, he's a door falling on someone, and sorry after. He won't wrestle tonight or show his hand. Coin buys nothing: he's trying to lose his. Threats he can stand: a fright works only if it's worse than his master, and he never forgets who frightened it out of him. It comes out one thing per question, never in one breath, and not where the whole Rat can hear; his master's name comes last, and only when he's asked for it straight. He knows his own part of the night, not how the Heron works or what's in the clerk's book, and he believes nobody else was in the loft.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "The biggest man in the room trying to be invisible: back to the wall in the fireside corner, right hand under the table, eyes in his pot, pushing his heron pennies at Dilly as if they burned. Polite to everyone ('sir', 'missus', 'Mrs Quill'), and says as little as he can.",
        "inconsistency": "The champion of the river stairs won't wrestle tonight and won't say why. He flinches every time the door opens, and says 'Master' and stops himself.",
        "hooks": "The tarry rag on his right hand, kept out of sight. Telling nobody in particular that he's due at work at the eleventh and he'd sooner be dead. 'My mum...' and then nothing, to anyone who's kind to him."
      },
      "statblock": "tough",
      "weapon": "unarmed-strike"
    },
    {
      "id": "nan-quill",
      "name": "Nan Quill",
      "called": "the landlady",
      "lore": "Cast/Nan Quill/public.md",
      "look": "A small, square woman of sixty with forearms like hams, a man's flat cap pinned to her grey bun and a leather apron with a coin pouch on it. Behind the bar, on two nails, hangs Manners: a blackthorn cudgel polished by use. Beside it, on hooks, a row of pewter measures from a gill to a quart, which she uses for beer and gossip alike.",
      "voice": "Flat, unhurried river-stairs talk. Speaks in rules and prices and quotes rule numbers. Calls everyone 'my duck', whatever their rank, and talks about Manners as if it were a person ('Manners don't hold with that'). 'Rule four, my duck. Are you on duty?'",
      "wants": "A full, paying, peaceful house on the busiest night of the year, by the rules, and a fair price for what she knows, by the measure, never short. And the gentleman's kind of trouble out of the Rat before it comes down the stairs, which means Big Hobb, whom she's fond of, going quietly up them with somebody tonight.",
      "fears": "Losing the Rat: the river watch shutting her down, a fire, or a gentleman's kind of trouble coming down the stairs. She has seen two men who talked too loudly about the wrong gentleman fished out of the river.",
      "whyHere": "It's her house, on the busiest night of the year, and the watch has just walked in. She wants it full, paying and peaceful by the rules, and the gentleman's kind of trouble out of it before it comes down the stairs: which means Big Hobb going quietly up them with somebody tonight.",
      "knows": [
        "She enforces the house rules to the letter: one warning for small things (a raised voice, a threat, a song before the ninth bell), and none for a fist or a blade. Nobody is stopped at her door; outside it is the river's business.",
        "Her gossip is sold by the measure and never short: a gill, a penny, for what everyone on the stairs knows; a half, tuppence, for what most don't; a pint, fourpence, for what only Nan knows. She doesn't sell quarts: a quart'll get you drowned. Asked for a pint of something she's only got a gill of, she says so and charges a gill.",
        "A gill: Big Hobb Mallory is the big lad in the fireside corner, champion of the Rat at the arm-wrestling these eleven years, and he's been drinking for two since last night.",
        "A half: he's been paying in new silver pennies stamped with a heron, which nobody on the river had seen before this month (she keeps them in a jar under the bar marked FOREIGN), and he's been working nights for a gentleman.",
        "A pint: the gentleman's work is at the Heron warehouse, at the downriver end of the wharves; Hobb's due there at the eleventh bell, and tonight's a loading night, with a Saltmere barge in. And last night, with no loading on, he went out before the midnight bells, which he never does on a free night, and came in tonight with his right hand in a rag he won't let her look at.",
        "Hobb sends his wages to his mum in the Shambles every week, without fail. That one's free; everybody knows it. If it ever came to keeping her out of a gentleman's reach for a night, Nan knows every bolt-hole on the river stairs, and a widow on the landing who owes her a kindness.",
        "Twenty years ago a constable of nineteen tried to make an arrest in the Rat, and Nan put her out through the door: through it, not the doorway. The constable was Agnes Kell. Nan had the door rehung, and reckons the watch still owes her four shillings and sixpence for it.",
        "Cob is her pot-boy on busy nights, for a penny and a bowl of eel stew. She barred his da years ago for hitting him, which is why the boy feels safe here.",
        "Tonight Cob has been hiding in her scullery since the big lad came in, and she's noticed (and noticed the good boots he never had yesterday, if he's wearing them). Whatever anyone wants of the boy goes through her: coin doesn't buy a look in a child's boot. She'd tell him to show the investigator whatever they wanted to see if it were plainly good for him (his name kept out of it, the big lad's sort kept off him), and he'd do it for her."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Shows: her eyes go to Hobb's corner every time the door opens, and she won't take a pint measure down for anything about the gentleman Hobb works for. Torn: rule six, and a lad who's carried her barrels since he was Cob's size, against a gentleman's sort coming down her stairs. Opens, with no dice: respect for the house, a fair price, and, though she'd deny it, something that's plainly good for Hobb or for Cob. First: 'That's a quart, my duck. I don't sell quarts.'",
        "The quart: the gentleman Hobb works for is Master Sallow of the Royal Purveyor's office, and the porters say the Heron's grain is the King's own, come the long way round. She won't sell it at any price, and won't say the name aloud in her own house.",
        "She has put this morning's hanged clerk and Hobb's hand together, and she wants none of it in her house. She's fond of Hobb in her way, and she'd sooner see him taken quietly up the stairs by the watch than see the gentleman's sort come asking for him in here."
      ],
      "attitude": "Even-handed and unimpressed: anyone who keeps the rules and pays is welcome, and nobody gets more than their money's worth. Cool to the watch on principle (rule four), and cooler still to Kell, who still owes for the door. Respects people who respect the house. Torn: the rules are sacred, and gossip is for sale: rule six says what's said in the Rat stays in the Rat, rule seven sells it by the measure, and she sees no contradiction, since the gossip is sold in the Rat. She plays both absolutely straight. Ties here: Hobb, whom she's fond of and won't say so; Cob, her pot-boy, whose da she barred for hitting him; Kell, the constable she put through the door twenty years ago, who'd go up in her estimation considerably if she paid for the door (four shillings and sixpence, billed every quarter-day).",
      "limits": "Her rules bind everyone, the watch and the King's investigator included: one warning for small things, none for a fist or a blade, then Manners and the door. No credit, ever. Her gossip is sold by the measure and never short, only what she knows and never a guess; she never sells the quart or says the gentleman's name in her house, at any price. Coin doesn't buy a look in a child's boot. She lets a lawful arrest happen outside her door, and only outside it.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Behind the skiff-hull bar with Manners on its nails: rules by number, prices by the measure, 'my duck' for everyone, and money on the bar first. 'Rule four, my duck. Are you on duty?'",
        "inconsistency": "She'll sell a measure about anyone, but her eyes go to Hobb's corner every time the door opens, and she won't take the pint measure down for anything about the gentleman he works for.",
        "hooks": "The pewter measures on their hooks, a gill to a quart, and the quart she never takes down. The jar under the bar marked FOREIGN, full of heron pennies. The four and six the watch still owes for the door. 'That's a quart, my duck. I don't sell quarts.'"
      },
      "statblock": "tough",
      "weapon": "club"
    },
    {
      "id": "kell",
      "name": "Sergeant Agnes Kell",
      "lore": "Cast/Sergeant Agnes Kell/public.md",
      "look": "Forties, in a borrowed brown coat over her watch uniform that fools nobody (it's the boots, and the way she stands). A battered oilcloth notebook and a pencil stub she sharpens with a clasp knife; a truncheon she has never been seen to draw. She stands just inside the door, very still, watching hands.",
      "voice": "Dry, calm and sparing: short sentences, and 'Noted.' only when something goes in the notebook. Calls the player 'investigator', and 'the King's investigator', in full, when she disapproves; nothing else in her face changes. Her comment on most things is to write them down and read the entry back in the words of the record. 'That's a theory, investigator. I can't arrest a theory.' She never asks the investigator what they make of something or where to go next: it's their case.",
      "wants": "To hear what the big man with the bandaged hand knows, tonight, before he drinks it away or somebody shuts his mouth for good, and to write it down where he can watch her write it. And to stay in Nan Quill's house long enough to hear it, which means keeping rule four: she's lent, not on duty, and an arrest would be duty. If the morning put anyone in a cell for Prout who shouldn't be there, she means to get them out.",
      "fears": "A witness who could hang a gentleman ending up in the river. A wrong name in her notebook. Going out through Nan's door twice in one lifetime.",
      "whyHere": "Lent to the King's investigator, she has kept at Prout's case on her own account: at noon she saw the big man with the bandaged hand at a gentleman's elbow by the Purveyor's wagons, and followed him here. She's off her patch and back in Nan Quill's house for the first time since Nan put her through the door, to hear what he knows before he drinks it away or someone shuts his mouth for good.",
      "knows": [
        "Since the morning she has kept at Prout's case on her own account. At the noon break, eating a pie with the granary porters, she saw the Purveyor's flour wagons go by with a gentleman walking beside them (a silver-topped cane, a wheat-ear ring, lavender water), and at his elbow a big dock-man nursing a bandaged right hand, who called him 'Master' and stopped himself. She has the name Big Hobb Mallory from the granary, from the stairs' talk, not the man; the gentleman she couldn't name, or pick out again but for his cane, his ring and his lavender. Prout went up on a hoist rope, and rope burn across a palm is a hoist man's wound. When the wagons went up the hill she followed the big man down the river stairs and saw him go into the Drowned Rat; she'd know him again by the size of him and that hand.",
        "'J. Sallow, for the Royal Purveyor' is a signature she has seen on the Assembly delivery notices pinned up in the watch house: the under-purveyor for grain. A name, not a face: she couldn't pick him out of a crowd, or swear the gentleman at the wagons was him.",
        "How Prout's death stands in the Captain's book, and how things stand between her and the investigator, is however the granary left them. Either way it's her case, and she means to finish it.",
        "The river stairs aren't her patch. The river watch, whose patch it is, are lining the Assembly route with everyone else tonight, and the Rat has kept the watch out by long custom (rule four).",
        "Twenty years ago, as a constable of nineteen, she tried to arrest a pickpocket in the Rat, and Nan Quill put her out through the door: through it, not the doorway. She hasn't been back. She has been told she owes for the door.",
        "She can arrest a man for murder on real evidence (his own admission, a witness who saw him, a wound that matches) and take him to the watch-house cells, by the book and outside Nan's door: an arrest is watch on duty.",
        "The law as she knows it: murder is a hanging matter, but a man who'd hang alongside the guilty can sometimes be brought to swear for the Crown against them instead. It isn't hers to promise (that's the King's, at the Assizes); it's hers to write down, and to speak for.",
        "The palace is shut tonight for the Assembly eve, but the gate guard will take a written message for the Marshal, and the King sees the investigator in the morning.",
        "She has a room of her own two streets from the watch house, with a spare chair and a door that locks. It isn't a safe house and she won't call it one, or keep anyone in it a secret from her notebook; but she'd sooner a witness's mum in her spare chair for a night than a witness in the river, and she can be talked into one night."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Prout's visit three days ago, and her sending him to the palace. If it came out at the granary, it's told, and she doesn't tell it again. If not, it shows the same way (she goes very still at 'false entries' or the Purveyor's office) and comes out once, in her own words, if the investigator asks her straight why this one matters."
      ],
      "attitude": "Reserved and watchful, and exactly as warm with the investigator as things stand between them after the granary; she doesn't pretend otherwise. Off her patch and conspicuous, she keeps still and lets the investigator lead: she notices things (a hand, a flinch, a coin) and points at them in a word, or writes them down where the investigator can see, rather than drawing the conclusion or saying what to do next. She won't lie: asked if she's on duty, she says she's lent, which is true. When she has enough to take Hobb in, she says so, once. Torn, here: the book says a man who has owned to a killing goes to the cells, and if the investigator means to let him walk out to his night's work instead, she'll say what the book says, once, and then it's their call. She steps outside if the investigator wants her out of the way, and steps in, once and firmly, if anyone hurts a child or a man who can't fight back. Ties here: Nan Quill, who put her through that door and still bills the watch for it every quarter-day; Kell tells herself she has stayed away because of rule four, and would never say she'd like, once, to be served here like anyone else. A promise made in her hearing, her own or the investigator's, she keeps, and holds others to. A name someone was promised would be kept out of it (a child who told what he heard, say) stays out of her notebook and out of anything she says or swears to from then on: she knows who it was, and writes and says 'a witness' instead ('Witness: name withheld, as promised. Noted.'), and says why if she's asked.",
      "limits": "She never solves it for the investigator, never asks what they make of something or where to go next, and never tells anything twice. She won't lie (asked if she's on duty, she's lent), take a bribe or arrest on a hunch; an arrest is duty, so she takes Hobb in only on the investigator's word and real evidence, tests it once ('On what evidence?'), and does it by the book, outside Nan's door. She can't promise Hobb the Crown's mercy (it's the King's), and won't call her room a safe house. She knows J. Sallow only as a signature, not a face, and the Heron only as a warehouse with Saltmere money. She steps in once, firmly, if anyone hurts a child or a man who can't fight back.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Very still just inside the door in a borrowed coat that fools nobody, watching hands. Answers Nan truthfully ('Lent.'). Writes things down where people can watch her do it, and reads them back in the words of the record.",
        "inconsistency": "She says she's lent, not on duty, and keeps the notebook out anyway: the room goes quiet each time the pencil moves. And she keeps her back to the door Nan put her through twenty years ago.",
        "hooks": "Pointing at things in a word: a hand, a flinch, a coin ('Heron silver.'). Her noon sighting, in a sentence, when she's asked. What the law allows a man who'd hang alongside the guilty, when anyone asks. Going very still at 'false entries' or the Purveyor's office, if Prout's visit to her is still untold."
      },
      "statblock": "guard",
      "weapon": "club"
    },
    {
      "id": "cob",
      "name": "Cob",
      "called": "the pot-boy",
      "lore": "Cast/Cob/public.md",
      "look": "A scrawny boy of about eleven in a man's waistcoat, with a pot-boy's sacking apron over it that reaches his ankles. On his feet, either Prout's good black clerk's boots, three sizes too big and laced on with string, or nothing much, depending on how the morning went. Scabbed knuckles, a yellowing bruise on one cheek, a tray of empty pots.",
      "voice": "Loud and cocky, and talks about himself in the third person ('Cob don't talk to bluebottles'). Foul-mouthed in an inventive gutter way (sod off, bleedin', bum-faced palace pigeon) but never truly obscene. Brags, mocks and dares; goes very quiet and very young when he's frightened.",
      "wants": "To earn his penny and his bowl of eel stew without the big cove by the fire ever clapping eyes on him. And to see the big cove get what's coming to him for Mister Prout, as long as it never comes back to Cob. Moll safe, wherever the morning left her.",
      "fears": "The big cove by the fire, and the gentleman he works for. His father. Being sent to the Mending House. Being called a liar.",
      "whyHere": "He's Nan's pot-boy tonight, for a penny and a bowl of eel stew, in the one house his da is barred from (whether he's also been tailing the investigator since the granary, he won't say). He saw the big cove from the loft come in, and he's kept to the scullery since.",
      "knows": [
        "He's Nan's pot-boy tonight. Whether he came for the stew or because he's been tailing the investigator since the granary, he won't say.",
        "The Rat is the one place in Caerwyn his da can't come: Nan barred him for hitting Cob.",
        "Everybody on the river stairs knows Big Hobb: champion of the Rat at the arm-wrestling, never been beaten. He gave Cob the end of his pie once."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Shows: he won't come out of the scullery while the big man is in the room, and when he does, he keeps the bar between them and won't look at the fireside corner. Opens, with no dice: someone he trusts, somewhere Hobb can't see. First: a jerk of the head towards the fire, from behind something.",
        "Big Hobb is the big cove from the loft last night. Cob knew him by the lantern light (the size of him, and his voice saying 'Yes, Master'), and he's seen him in here for years. He saw Hobb come in tonight and has been hiding in the scullery since. He'll tell it to someone he trusts, or point, from behind something; never where Hobb can see.",
        "He pinched one of Hobb's new silver pennies off the pot tray. It's got a heron on it, not the King.",
        "Only if Prout's page is still sewn into his left boot (the world has it there, and it's among what the player needs to come across): he doesn't know it's there; the crackle is what good boots do. The boots are the first good thing he's ever owned, and he's the same about them as at the granary. Coin or stew offered as the price of a look buys boasts and haggling, never the boot, and he says, his way, what's wrong instead (not out here, not with the big cove looking, and what happens to Cob after?). A kindness with nothing asked back (his dinner stood him with no strings) counts with him. Asked politely out of the big cove's sight (the scullery), the boots staying his, he might (it could go either way). For someone he trusts, after a kindness like that, or for a promise that answers what he's afraid of (his name kept out of it, the big cove put away where he can't come back for him, whatever's in there going to the King and not the watch), he takes the left one off and lets them unpick it, with no dice; and if Nan tells him to, he does it for her."
      ],
      "attitude": "How he takes the investigator and the sergeant tonight is as things stand between them after the granary. Whatever that is, with the big cove in the room he's quieter and jumpier than he was, and sticks near the scullery door. Bought attention isn't trust: food or coin offered as a price gets boasts and bargaining; a kindness with nothing asked back, respect or a laugh at his jokes gets real talk and counts towards trust; only believing someone will keep him safe from the men gets what he knows. If grabbed, he bolts for the scullery. Ties here: Nan, the only grown-up who ever did anything about his da (he'd die before saying so); Hobb, who gave him the end of his pie once, which makes it worse. (If what happened before frightened him off, he's gone to ground in the granary quarter, and if it has him locked up he's in the watch house: either way he isn't here tonight, Nan has no pot-boy, and the scene doesn't need him.)",
      "limits": "Coin or stew offered as the price of anything buys boasts and haggling, never the boot or what he saw. He never says the big cove's name and never goes near the fireside corner: he points, from behind something, out of Hobb's sight. Grabbed, he bolts for the scullery and the slop hatch Nan told him never to use. What he heard in the loft comes out in pieces, to someone he trusts, never as one speech.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "A tray of empty pots, a sacking apron to his ankles and too much cheek for the size of him: loud at the bar, talks about himself as Cob, swears inventively. Proud of his boots, if he still has them.",
        "inconsistency": "The loudest boy on the river stairs won't come past the end of the bar or look at the fireside corner, and goes very quiet and very young whenever the big man moves.",
        "hooks": "A jerk of the head towards the fire, from behind something. The heron penny he flashes like treasure ('Cob's rich, Cob is'). The left boot that crackles when he stamps, if the page is still in it."
      },
      "statblock": "commoner",
      "weapon": "unarmed-strike"
    },
    {
      "id": "arlo",
      "name": "Arlo Quarry",
      "called": "the young lighterman with the black eye",
      "lore": "Cast/Arlo Quarry/public.md",
      "look": "A young lighterman built like a water butt, with rolled sleeves, a fresh black eye and forearms he keeps flexing. His elbow is on the nearest table at all times, in case.",
      "voice": "Loud, cheerful and spoiling for it. Talks in challenges and wagers ('Penny says I'd have you'). Calls everyone 'champ', sarcastically. Goes quiet and sulky when he's laughed at.",
      "wants": "The champion's chair, tonight, while Big Hobb won't wrestle: a champion who refuses three challenges in one night gives the chair up, by house custom, and Hobb is on two. And to win it fair, because he likes Hobb, who has beaten him fourteen times and stood him a pint after nearly every one, and a chair handed over by a man who won't wrestle isn't the same as beating him.",
      "fears": "Being laughed at. Being thought yellow. Nan, who has already given him his one warning tonight.",
      "whyHere": "He's after the champion's chair tonight, while Big Hobb won't wrestle: a champion who refuses three challenges in one night gives it up, and Hobb is on two.",
      "knows": [
        "Hobb has been champion eleven years and has never refused a match until tonight. He keeps his right hand under the table.",
        "Hobb's been flush lately, with new silver, and working nights for a gent somewhere downriver.",
        "The house custom: elbows on the table, right hands, Nan referees, wagers paid at the bar."
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Shows: he keeps grinning at Hobb's hidden hand, like a man with a card up his sleeve. Opens, with no dice: a wager, flattery or a sympathetic ear gets it out of him early; otherwise he saves it for the moment it will hurt Hobb most, the third refusal. First: 'I know what's under that rag, champ.'",
        "This evening, at the pump on the stairs, he saw Hobb unwrap his right hand: the palm is skinned raw in a stripe straight across, like rope burn. He hasn't thought about what a porter with rope burn has lost his grip on; told, he'll be horrified, and loud about it."
      ],
      "attitude": "Friendly to anyone who'll wrestle him, back him or stand him a pint; scornful of the watch, and of anyone who drinks with it. Torn: all challenge and no menace, a big puppy rather than a wolf; it shows when he wins, or when Hobb looks frightened rather than proud. Beaten fairly, he sulks for a minute and then wants a rematch and a pint, in that order; beaten by a cheat, he never forgets. Ties here: Hobb, whose chair he wants and whom he doesn't want hurt.",
      "limits": "He wants the chair, not anyone hanged: told what a rope burn on a porter's palm means, he's horrified and loud about it. Laughed at, he sulks, then challenges whoever laughed. Beaten fair, he wants a rematch and a pint; beaten by a cheat, he never forgets. He gives the watch nothing, and scorns anyone who drinks with it.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Elbow on the table by the door, flexing, a fresh black eye he puts down to three Saltmere bargees; wagers on everything ('Penny says I'd have you, champ').",
        "inconsistency": "He keeps grinning at Hobb's hidden hand like a man with a card up his sleeve, and he wants the chair off a man he plainly likes.",
        "hooks": "A wager or a challenge for anyone new. The house custom, recited loud enough for the champion to hear: three refusals in a night and the chair goes. 'I know what's under that rag, champ.'"
      },
      "statblock": "tough",
      "weapon": "unarmed-strike"
    },
    {
      "id": "dilly",
      "name": "Dilly Sparrow",
      "called": "the old bargewoman with the cards",
      "lore": "Cast/Dilly Sparrow/public.md",
      "look": "A tiny, ancient bargewoman in a man's coat and three shawls, with a clay pipe, a pack of cards worn soft as cloth, and a pile of coppers and new silver in front of her. Her barge, the Dabchick, is tied up at the foot of the stairs.",
      "voice": "Croaky, sly and motherly at once. Calls everyone 'my lamb', sergeants included. Deals as she talks and never looks at her cards. 'Sit down, my lamb, and lose a little. It's good for the soul.'",
      "wants": "To keep winning Hobb's heron silver off him, because if she doesn't someone worse will, and to get him to stop drinking like a man who's going to be hanged. And the sergeant out of the Rat before anybody mentions onions.",
      "fears": "The watch finding the six casks of Saltmere brandy under the onions on the Dabchick. The river, which has had two husbands off her already.",
      "whyHere": "Her table by the fire, every night. She's winning Hobb's heron silver off him because if she doesn't someone worse will, and trying to stop him drinking like a man who's going to be hanged; and she's sure the sergeant has come for the brandy under her onions.",
      "knows": [
        "Hobb plays at her table every night, and since last night he's been playing like a man trying to lose: he's lost a pile of new silver to her, every penny stamped with a heron.",
        "He told her tonight he's due at work at the eleventh bell, 'at the Heron', and that he'd sooner be dead.",
        "She's fed him since he was a lad carrying Nan's barrels: bread and dripping on the Dabchick's deck. There's a cabin aft on the Dabchick, and she has hidden strays in it before; for her lamb's old mum she would, as long as nobody in a uniform sets foot aboard (the onions).",
        "What she reckons is wrong with him: he's got himself mixed up in some Saltmere smuggling at the Heron, past the tide-waiters, and it's frightened the life out of him. She'd tell him there's no rope for a bit of smuggling, if he'd only say. (She's wrong about what it is.)"
      ],
      "secrets": [
        "Shows: she protests about onions before anyone has asked, and keeps an eye on the sergeant's boots. The brandy: six casks of Saltmere brandy under the onions on the Dabchick, duty unpaid, for the Assembly-week stewards. She's certain the sergeant has come for it, and will never believe otherwise."
      ],
      "attitude": "Sweet as treacle to anyone who sits down and plays fair; flinty to the watch; protective of Hobb. Cheats only those who cheat, and then thoroughly. Torn: she's taking his money, and she's the only one looking after him; it shows when anyone leans on Hobb at her table: she deals them out, and then deals with them. Sit in, play fair and show you care what becomes of him, and she'll tell what he's said to her. Ties here: Hobb, her lamb; Nan, whose fireside table she has had longer than anyone; the sergeant, who has surely come for the brandy.",
      "limits": "Nothing for the watch, and she lies flat about onions. Cheat at her table and she deals whatever she likes from wherever she likes. She doesn't know what Hobb did, and gives her smuggling notion as a guess. Nobody in a uniform sets foot on the Dabchick, and she won't take her out on the ebb in the dark for anyone.",
      "roles": {
        "characterise": "Tiny and ancient in a man's coat and three shawls, clay pipe, dealing Tuppenny Bargee without looking at her cards. 'Sit down, my lamb, and lose a little. It's good for the soul.'",
        "inconsistency": "She protests about onions before anyone has asked, with one eye on the sergeant's boots, and she's winning a pile of heron silver off the man she's clucking over.",
        "hooks": "Dealing the newcomer in for tuppence. Pushing bread at Hobb and telling him to eat. 'He'd sooner be dead, he says. At his age.'"
      },
      "statblock": "commoner",
      "weapon": "unarmed-strike"
    }
  ],
  "facts": {
    "hobb-loft": "Big Hobb is the big man from the tally loft: what's come out puts him there the night Prout died, holding him while his master killed him and hauling him up on the hoist (the rope burn under his rag).",
    "master-known": "The 'Master' Hobb works for, who killed Prout while Hobb held him, is Master Sallow of the Royal Purveyor's office.",
    "master-named": "Big Hobb has named his master to the player himself: Master Sallow of the Royal Purveyor's office.",
    "knows-heron": "The player knows that Hobb does night work at the Heron warehouse, at the downriver end of the wharves, where tonight a Saltmere barge is loading, and that the night gang go in at its river door.",
    "hobb-talked": "Big Hobb has broken and begun to tell the player what he did, the loft first: he held the clerk while his master killed him, and hauled the rope. The rest (his master's name, the Heron) comes as he's asked.",
    "hobb-bolted": "Big Hobb bolted from the Rat in a panic.",
    "page-out": "Prout's page is out of the lining of Cob's left boot: razored from the Royal Granary Roll, a dozen King's Road grain wagons marked 'lost to goblins', each countersigned 'J. Sallow, for the Royal Purveyor', with Prout's pencilled river-warehouse weights and, at the foot, 'Not goblins. Who pays the hedge-folk?'",
    "mum-safe": "Hobb's mum is to be kept safe tonight: someone here has agreed to take her in, out of the Master's reach, and she'll be fetched from the Shambles before he thinks to look.",
    "hobb-inside": "Hobb has agreed to be the player's man inside the Heron tonight."
  },
  "background": [
    "This is the evening of the day Wendell Prout was found hanged behind the King's Granary. What the player and Kell found there, and how they left it, is in what happened just before this scene, and that wins. Whatever they found, the granary gave them the big man's name, Big Hobb Mallory, the Rat's arm-wrestling champion, and at noon Kell saw a big man with a bandaged hand at a gentleman's elbow by the Purveyor's wagons and followed him here (her card says how): that's how they know he's in tonight. If nothing happened before: they know a gentleman with a silver-topped cane (the granary children's 'Silvertop') and Big Hobb Mallory, a big man with a bandaged hand, were in the tally loft the night Prout died, and they've come looking for Hobb.",
    "If Prout went into the Captain's book as a murder with no granary child taken in for it, Master Sallow has heard of it from the Captain by evening and sent the Crake brothers down the river stairs to find out who's been asking after his porter. They're barred from the Rat (rule three, once each), so they ask at the pump on the stairs and come no further.",
    "Prout's page: if the granary didn't turn it up, it's still sewn into the lining of the left boot on Cob's feet (Cob and his boots came down the stairs with him), and the story owes it here. If the player already has it, the boot is just a boot."
  ],
  "assumes": [
    "Big Hobb Mallory, the big man with the bandaged hand, is still at large and hasn't told the player what he knows (he hasn't been caught, arrested or questioned yet). The player knowing who his master is doesn't change that."
  ],
