  "afterwards": [],
  "fronts": "World/Fronts.md",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": -138,
        "y": 510
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "start",
        "title": "Rules of the house",
        "description": "This scene is the second leg of the investigation, and it's about one man. Hours after the granary, the trail of the big man with the bandaged hand has brought the player and Kell down the river stairs to the Drowned Rat, and the whole river is in tonight: the docks shut tomorrow for the procession.\n\nIntroduce the Rat, its rules and its people, and let the room react to the watch walking in. What Hobb did in the loft, who he works for and where he goes at the bell are discoveries for play, not opening exposition.\n\nOpening situation:\n- Open at the Rat's door: the bunting on the quay above, the river slapping at the foot of the stairs, the din inside. As they step down into the room the din drops by half: the regulars have made Kell for watch before she's through the door (it's the boots).\n- Show what a first glance would, by look rather than by name: the long smoky room, the rules board just inside the door, a small square landlady behind the bar with a cudgel to hand, an ancient bargewoman dealing cards by the fire, a young lighterman by the door with his elbow on a table, a great many big men with a great many bandages between them, and the biggest of them all in the fireside corner, back to the wall, one hand out of sight. The player knows the name Big Hobb Mallory, not his face: nobody here gets a name until someone says it.\n- Cob stays out of it: he's in the scullery, out of the big man's sight, and comes out with his tray only once the newcomers have been here a little while. (If the granary frightened him off or put him in the cells, he isn't here: Nan has no pot-boy tonight.)\n- The eleventh bell, when the night work on the wharves starts, is the clock: Hobb has to be at work when it rings. Let it be felt coming, a step nearer every few exchanges (the clock says how).\n\nSuggested ways in:\n- Go straight to the biggest man by the fire. He's polite, frightened and closed, and wants his pot and to be left alone: the hand stays under the table, he flinches at the door, and he says 'Master' and stops himself. Just asking gets 'sir' and silence; what gets through is something that answers his fear.\n- Answer Nan and rule four: she asks the sergeant whether she's on duty. Buy a drink, or a measure of gossip (a gill, a half, a pint, money on the bar first).\n- Sit in at Dilly's game, tuppence a hand, and play fair: she's protective of Hobb, and talks to anyone who shows they care what becomes of him.\n- Take Arlo up on a wager, or watch him: he wants Hobb's chair tonight, and keeps grinning at the hidden hand.\n- The pot-boy, once he's out: he keeps the bar between him and the fireside corner, and won't look at it.\n\nPlay the rules absolutely straight: rule four makes Kell a problem the whole Rat expects someone to solve, rule one means money on the bar, and Nan gives one warning and then it's Manners. The regulars talk to anyone who keeps the house rules, plays, pays or wins, and dry up whenever Kell's notebook comes out. Everyone here is a door, not a wall: answer what the player tries with a real reaction and a way forward, without giving secrets away for nothing. Kell lets the investigator lead: she notices things and points at them in a word, writes them down, and never says what to do next.\n\nArlo's challenges, and how they count: Hobb has refused him twice tonight already, before the player came in (the room knows it: 'Hobb's on two'). Arlo's next challenge is the third, and it's an event of its own, Arlo's third challenge [arlos-challenge]: until it comes, Arlo wagers, needles the room, recites the custom and grins at the hidden hand, but he doesn't put his elbow on Hobb's table. It comes once, and whatever comes of it (Hobb wrestles, the player takes Arlo on, someone stops it), it's done. If Hobb refuses it, that's his third refusal, and by the house custom it costs him the champion's chair there and then: Nan says so out loud, Hobb gets up out of it, and nobody counts again. There's no fourth challenge.\n\nAn opening image to use or adapt: the din drops by half as the boots come down the three steps, every eye goes to the sergeant, and in the fireside corner the biggest man in the room puts his face in his pot. End on Nan, with a look at the rules board, asking the sergeant whether she's on duty.\n\nThese are example openings, not a required sequence. Follow whichever person, detail or approach the player engages with, and let the discoveries come out in any order.",
        "cast": {
          "hobb": "Characterise: as invisible as a man the size of a door can be. Back to the wall in the fireside corner, right hand under the table, eyes in his pot, losing at Dilly's cards on purpose. Flinches when the door opens. 'Sir' and silence to anyone who asks him anything.",
          "nan-quill": "Characterise: the rules and the prices. Points the newcomers at the board, asks the sergeant whether she's on duty, and sells what's asked for at the posted measure, money on the bar first. One warning for a raised voice.",
          "kell": "Characterise: very still just inside the door, watching hands. Answers Nan truthfully ('Lent.'). Lets the investigator lead; points at things in a word, and writes them down where people can watch.",
          "arlo": "Characterise: elbow on the table by the door, flexing; a wager or a challenge for the newcomers ('Penny says I'd have you, champ'). Grins at Hobb's hidden hand and talks about the chair (Hobb's on two refusals), but hasn't made his third challenge yet. Nothing for the watch.",
          "dilly": "Characterise: deals Tuppenny Bargee by the fire without looking at her cards, calls everyone 'my lamb', protests about onions before anyone has asked, and keeps an eye on the sergeant's boots.",
          "cob": "In the scullery, out of the big man's sight. Comes out with his tray only once the newcomers have been here a little while, keeps the bar between him and the fireside corner, and takes the investigator as things stand between them after the granary."
        },
        "lore": [
          "Places/The Drowned Rat.md"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "the-loft",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 420,
        "y": 0
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "The big man from the loft",
        "description": "The player comes to know that Big Hobb is the big man from the tally loft, and what he did there. What it substantiates depends on the route: the rope burn under the rag is a wound that matches; Cob's account is a witness's (a child's, who heard more than he saw, in the dark); Hobb's own words, written in Kell's notebook as he said them, are an admission that will stand anywhere. Kell takes a man in on his own admission, or on a witness with the wound set beside it. Getting his own words is the heart of the scene: see the event 'I only held the rope' [hobb-talking]. These are example paths, not a sequence: other plausible approaches to the same revelation count.",
        "revelation": "Big Hobb is the big man from the tally loft: he held Prout's arms while his master struck and strangled him, then hauled him up on the sack hoist, and the rope took the skin off his right palm (that's what's under the rag).",
        "paths": [
          {
            "via": "hobb",
            "clue": "His own words, haltingly, the loft first: 'I only held him. I only held the rope.' He's been sick twice since.",
            "earn": "No dice: whatever truly answers his fear opens him, whether his mum kept out of the Master's reach tonight, a way out of the noose he can believe (swearing for the Crown, with Kell's word that she'll write it down and speak for it), or a kindness he can't stand up to. Never where the whole Rat can hear.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "hobb",
            "clue": "The same admission, got out of him where it could go either way.",
            "earn": "An appeal, an argument or a bargain while he's still holding out (Persuasion DC 14; failing, he drinks and looks at the door, nearer leaving). A fright worse than his master: the noose, the watch, looming over him (Intimidation DC 15; it comes out in a rush, and he never forgets who frightened it out of him; failing, he bolts). A lie he swallows, like being sent by his master to see he's kept quiet (Deception DC 13; he tells it as proof he's kept faith, the same truth; seen through, he bolts).",
            "strength": "strong",
            "check": {
              "skill": "persuasion",
              "dc": 14
            }
          },
          {
            "via": "cob",
            "clue": "The big cove from the loft: Cob knew him by lantern light, the size of him and his voice saying 'Yes, Master'; after the hoist stopped creaking he was shaking his right hand and wrapping it in a rag. Cob has seen him in here for years.",
            "earn": "No dice: only for someone Cob trusts, somewhere Hobb can't see (the scullery), first a jerk of the head towards the fire from behind something, then the rest in pieces. Say Hobb's name to him and his face answers; he never says it himself.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "arlo",
            "clue": "At the pump on the stairs this evening he saw Hobb unwrap his right hand: the palm skinned raw in a stripe straight across, like rope burn.",
            "earn": "No dice: a wager, flattery or a sympathetic ear gets it out of him early; otherwise he saves it for Hobb's third refusal, and says it in front of everyone. Told what a rope burn on a porter means, he's horrified, and loud.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "Hobb's right hand",
            "clue": "Under the tarry rag the palm is skinned raw in a stripe straight across: the mark a hoist rope leaves on whoever hauls on it.",
            "earn": "No dice: at the arm-wrestling the rag comes loose as the arms go over, win or lose (Athletics DC 14 decides only who wins, and what the room thinks of it). A glimpse when he forgets and reaches for his pot or his cards with it is a roll (Perception DC 13). Grabbing his wrist is a fight, and rule three.",
            "strength": "suggestive",
            "check": {
              "skill": "perception",
              "dc": 13
            }
          },
          {
            "via": "nan-quill",
            "clue": "Last night, with no loading on, he went out before the midnight bells, which he never does on a free night, and came in tonight with his right hand in a rag he won't let her look at.",
            "earn": "No dice: part of her pint, fourpence on the bar, for what only she knows about Hobb.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          }
        ],
        "establishes": [
          "hobb-loft"
        ],
        "lore": [
          "Cast/Big Hobb Mallory/public.md"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "the-master",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 420,
        "y": 260
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "Who he calls Master",
        "description": "The player learns who Hobb works for: the 'Master' at whose elbow Kell saw him at noon, who killed Prout while Hobb held him. This is half the lead the story needs from here (the other half is the Heron): Hobb can't go off to work having talked, with it still unasked. (Taken in first, he tells it in the cells, and the story owes it on.) Hobb's own word, given in a whisper and written in Kell's notebook, is testimony against Sallow. His face at the name and Nan's refusal to sell it point at a name the player brings (Kell's, from the delivery notices, or one from the granary: the page's countersignature, Moll's 'Master Sallow', the Captain's order), and Kell's noon sighting puts the big man at the elbow of a gentleman of the Purveyor's office: together they make it near enough certain. These are example paths, not a sequence.",
        "revelation": "The gentleman Hobb works for and calls 'Master', who killed Prout while Hobb held him, is Master Sallow of the Royal Purveyor's office: the 'J. Sallow' who countersigned the 'lost' wagons.",
        "paths": [
          {
            "via": "hobb",
            "clue": "'The Master', and the name in a whisper with a look at the door: Master Sallow of the Royal Purveyor's office, who struck the clerk with the head of his cane and used the cord.",
            "earn": "No dice: once he's talking, asked for it straight. It costs him most, so it comes last.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "hobb",
            "clue": "Any mention of the loft, a silver-topped cane or the name Sallow turns him grey and still; he says 'Master' and stops himself.",
            "earn": "No dice: free to anyone who says the name or describes the cane in his hearing.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "nan-quill",
            "clue": "'That's a quart, my duck. I don't sell quarts,' with her eyes on Hobb's corner. She sells what she knows and says so when she knows less, so a quart she won't sell is a name she has.",
            "earn": "No dice: free to anyone who asks who Hobb works for, or puts the name to her. She never says it in her house, at any price.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "kell",
            "clue": "At noon she saw this big man at the elbow of a gentleman with a silver-topped cane, a wheat-ear signet ring and a great deal of lavender water, walking beside the Purveyor's flour wagons, and he called him 'Master' and stopped himself. The wheat-ear is the Purveyor's office's mark, and the flour was going up for the Assembly feast, whose delivery notices on the watch-house wall are signed 'J. Sallow, for the Royal Purveyor', the under-purveyor for grain. A name and a description: she can't put the one to the other's face.",
            "earn": "No dice: what she saw, in a sentence, when asked; the name on the notices when asked whose wagons those were. With a name from the granary as well, it's near enough certain.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "cob",
            "clue": "Silvertop, the posh cove with the silver-topped cane who comes with the Purveyor's wagons and opens the granary with his own key: the big cove called him 'Master' in the loft.",
            "earn": "No dice: Cob's trust, out of Hobb's sight. If grown-ups describe a gent with a silver cane or name a gentleman of the Purveyor's office, he knows who they mean.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "Prout's page",
            "clue": "'J. Sallow, for the Royal Purveyor', countersigned against every 'lost' wagon: the name behind the theft Prout died for.",
            "earn": "No dice: in hand, brought from the granary or out of Cob's boot tonight.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          }
        ],
        "required": true,
        "establishes": [
          "master-known"
        ],
        "carry": {
          "unlocks": "the name behind the loft and the Heron: the man to catch tonight",
          "later": "the Heron (the Master comes at the midnight bell) and the King's morning (a name the King can act on)"
        },
        "lore": [
          "Cast/Master Josiah Sallow/public.md"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "the-heron",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 420,
        "y": 520
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "Where the grain goes: the Heron",
        "description": "The player learns where Hobb goes at the bell, and where the King's 'lost' grain goes: the lead to the rest of the night, so the scene can't end well, or even half well, without it (unless the player goes out on Hobb's heels at the bell). Nan's pint is the surest, Dilly the warmest, the room the cheapest, and Hobb himself has the way in on a string round his neck. These are example paths, not a sequence: the Heron's silver crossing the bar is common knowledge, so anyone may put a heron penny and the warehouse together.",
        "revelation": "Hobb's night work is at the Heron warehouse, the last on the wharves where the lamps give out: tonight's a loading night with a Saltmere barge in, Hobb's due on its hoist at the eleventh bell, and the night gang get in at its river door off the plank walk. If the King's 'lost' grain goes anywhere, it goes through there.",
        "paths": [
          {
            "via": "nan-quill",
            "clue": "The pint: the gentleman's work is at the Heron warehouse, at the downriver end of the wharves; Hobb's due there at the eleventh bell, and tonight's a loading night, with a Saltmere barge in. (The half, tuppence: he's been paying in new heron pennies and working nights for a gentleman.)",
            "earn": "No dice: fourpence on the bar for a pint about Hobb's night work. Coin is all it takes; she never sells short.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "dilly",
            "clue": "He told her tonight he's due at work at the eleventh bell, 'at the Heron', and that he'd sooner be dead. The heron on his new pennies is the Heron factor's own mark, on its sign and its sacks; she guesses he's mixed up in smuggling there.",
            "earn": "No dice: sit in at her table, play fair, and show you care what becomes of him. Never with a sergeant at your shoulder.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "the regulars",
            "clue": "Dock talk: the Heron pays double for night loading and doesn't care for questions; its gang go in at the river door off the plank walk; Hep Grist, its forewoman, takes on any strong pair of hands on a short night, and tonight's short, with half the river drinking in here.",
            "earn": "No dice: keep the house rules and stand a round, sit in, or win at the arm-wrestling (anyone who wins has the room). They dry up when Kell's notebook comes out; with the room clammed up for the watch, getting them going is a roll (Persuasion DC 12).",
            "strength": "suggestive",
            "check": {
              "skill": "persuasion",
              "dc": 12
            }
          },
          {
            "via": "hobb",
            "clue": "His own part of the night: the Heron, re-sacking ('It's the King's grain, sir. Everyone knows. Nobody says.'); a loading night, a barge for Saltmere; due on the hoist at the eleventh bell, in at the river door off the plank walk with the brass heron token round his neck shown at the grille; the Master comes at the midnight bell to see the barge off.",
            "earn": "No dice: once he's talking, asked where he works or how a body gets in. The token's only brass and the gang know his face, so he gives it up if asked.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "hobb",
            "clue": "Stopped on his way out, or being taken: he has to be at the Heron's river door at the eleventh bell, or the Master will know something's wrong, and the Master knows where his mum lives.",
            "earn": "No dice: said once, at the door, to whoever stands in his way at the bell or puts a hand on him.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "the heron pennies",
            "clue": "New silver with a heron where the King's face should be: in the jar under the bar marked FOREIGN, in Dilly's pile, in Hobb's purse, and in Cob's pocket (he pinched one off the pot tray).",
            "earn": "No dice: free to a look at the bar or the card table; Cob shows his off to anyone he's warming to.",
            "strength": "hook"
          }
        ],
        "required": true,
        "establishes": [
          "knows-heron"
        ],
        "carry": {
          "unlocks": "the Heron tonight: where the grain goes, and where Hobb goes at the bell",
          "later": "the wharves and the Heron warehouse need the player at its doors before the barge sails on the ebb at the second bell after midnight",
          "things": [
            "heron-token"
          ]
        },
        "lore": [
          "Places/Heron Warehouse.md"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "way-out",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 420,
        "y": 780
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "What would turn him",
        "description": "The player comes to understand what holds Hobb, and what would open him without a fright: his fear for his mum and his fear of the noose need separate answers, and people in this room can give both. It's what the room's people can offer, unasked or for the asking, so the scene doesn't hang on a roll at Hobb's table. Once someone here agrees to take his mum in and he hears it, he starts to tell; once his fear is answered, he'll even go back into the Heron for them. These are example paths, not a sequence.",
        "revelation": "Hobb is more frightened for his mum than of the noose: the Master knows where she lives, in the Shambles. His mum kept out of the Master's reach tonight would turn him (Kell's spare chair for one night, the cabin aft on Dilly's Dabchick, or a widow on the landing who owes Nan a kindness), and so would a way out that isn't the rope: a man who'd hang alongside the guilty can sometimes swear for the Crown against them.",
        "paths": [
          {
            "via": "hobb",
            "clue": "'He knows where my mum lives.' He's never heard that a man can swear for the Crown.",
            "earn": "No dice: asked gently what he's afraid of, by someone he's warming to; or said once at the door when he goes at the bell.",
            "strength": "strong"
          },
          {
            "via": "nan-quill",
            "clue": "Hobb sends his wages to his mum in the Shambles every week, without fail; and if it came to keeping her out of a gentleman's reach for a night, Nan knows every bolt-hole on the stairs, and a widow on the landing who owes her a kindness.",
            "earn": "No dice: his mum is free, since everybody knows it, and the bolt-holes come for a reason plainly good for Hobb, not for money.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "dilly",
            "clue": "He's her lamb, and he told her he'd sooner be dead. She has hidden strays in the cabin aft on the Dabchick before, and would for his old mum, as long as nobody in a uniform sets foot aboard.",
            "earn": "No dice: sitting in, playing fair, and showing you care what becomes of him; never with Kell at your shoulder.",
            "strength": "suggestive"
          },
          {
            "via": "kell",
            "clue": "The law as she knows it: a man who'd hang alongside the guilty can sometimes be brought to swear for the Crown against them; it's the King's to grant at the Assizes, and hers to write down and speak for. And her spare chair, for one night, for a witness's mum ('One night. It's written down.').",
            "earn": "No dice: asking her what the law allows a man like him, or how to keep a witness out of the river. The chair takes talking into (Persuasion DC 12, where it could go either way).",
            "strength": "strong"
          }
        ],
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "prouts-page",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 420,
        "y": 1040
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "Prout's page, in Cob's boot",
        "description": "Only while Prout's page is still sewn into the lining of the left boot on Cob's feet (the world has it there: the granary didn't turn it up, and the story owes it here). If the player already has the page, this is settled: the boot is just a boot, so don't steer to it. Cob doesn't know it's there; the crackle is what good boots do. He's the same boy about his boots as at the granary, and with the big cove in the room the only place he'll even think about it is the scullery. What it substantiates: the page is documentary proof of the theft, in Sallow's own countersignature, and something the King can hold. These are example paths, not a sequence.",
        "revelation": "Sewn into the lining of Cob's left boot is the page Prout razored from the Royal Granary Roll: a dozen King's Road grain wagons this past month marked 'lost to goblins', each countersigned 'J. Sallow, for the Royal Purveyor', with Prout's pencilled river-warehouse weights and, at the foot, 'Not goblins. Who pays the hedge-folk?'",
        "paths": [
          {
            "via": "cob",
            "clue": "On an upturned pot in the scullery he unlaces the left boot and picks the lining open with a dirty thumbnail: a page folded small and gone soft at the corners. 'That's Mister Prout's writing. That's why it crackled.' He can't read it.",
            "earn": "No dice: out of the big cove's sight, a kindness with nothing asked back (his dinner stood him, no strings) and then a polite ask; or a promise that answers what he's afraid of (his name kept out of it, the big cove put away where he can't come back for him, the boots his to keep, what's in there going to the King and not the watch). A polite ask before he has reason to trust them could go either way (Persuasion DC 13). Coin or stew as the price of a look gets haggling, never the look.",
            "strength": "proof",
            "check": {
              "skill": "persuasion",
              "dc": 13
            }
          },
          {
            "via": "nan-quill",
            "clue": "'Cob. Here, my duck. Show them your boot.' And because it's Nan, he does, sulking.",
            "earn": "No dice: a reason plainly good for the boy (his name kept out of it, the big man's sort kept off him). A reason that's only good for the player could go either way (Persuasion DC 12). Paying her gets nothing: coin doesn't buy a look in a child's boot.",
            "strength": "proof",
            "check": {
              "skill": "persuasion",
              "dc": 12
            }
          },
          {
            "via": "Cob's left boot",
            "clue": "Prout's good black clerk's boots, three sizes too big and laced on with string, and the left one crackles when he stamps.",
            "earn": "No dice: free to anyone who looks at his feet or hears him stamp. Taking it off him by force can be done, and costs: he bolts for the slop hatch, Nan comes round the bar, Hobb looks up, and Kell steps in once.",
            "strength": "hook"
          }
        ],
        "establishes": [
          "page-out"
        ],
        "carry": {
          "unlocks": "Sallow's countersignature in writing, beside Prout's weights: the motive, and proof the King can hold",
          "later": "the King's morning needs something he can hold, and the page is Sallow's hand on every 'lost' wagon",
          "things": [
            "ledger-page"
          ]
        },
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "arlos-challenge",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 797,
        "y": 84
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "Arlo's third challenge",
        "description": "Arlo comes for his third challenge of the night: elbow down on Hobb's table in front of everyone, by the house custom (a champion who refuses three challenges in one night gives up the chair). Hobb won't wrestle, because the rag would come off, and won't say why; the Rat starts to jeer. It puts the hand in front of the room, and Hobb's pride against his fear. The player can take Arlo on themselves (to spare Hobb, or to win the room: the regulars talk to whoever wins), back a side, goad Hobb to the table (the rag comes loose as the arms go over, win or lose), or stop it. If Hobb refuses the third time he gives up the chair, and Arlo, robbed of a fair win, says what he saw at the pump in front of everyone, unless he's already told someone; Hobb goes grey and looks at the door. It happens once, whichever way it goes.",
        "cast": {
          "arlo": "Slaps his elbow down on Hobb's table and calls him out for the chair, loud enough for the whole Rat: his third challenge tonight. Wagers with anyone who'll back him. At the third refusal, unless he's already told it: 'I know what's under that rag, champ.'",
          "hobb": "'Not tonight, Arlo.' Won't bring the right hand out from under the table. At the third refusal he says 'Have it, then,' gets up out of the champion's place, and won't look at anyone.",
          "nan-quill": "Referees the custom to the letter and says it out loud: three refusals in a night and the chair goes. One warning for anything rougher than words.",
          "dilly": "Tells Arlo to leave her lamb be, and deals anyone who'll sit down in, to take the room's eyes off Hobb.",
          "kell": "Watches the hand under the table. Writes one word.",
          "cob": "Watches from the scullery door while the room looks at the fireside; with every eye on Hobb, he can get a word to someone he trusts, from behind something."
        },
        "pressure": "The tenth bell goes across the water. Hobb counts his last heron pennies onto Dilly's table, looks at the door, and tells nobody in particular that he's due at work at the eleventh, and he'd sooner be dead.",
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "hobb-talking",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 860,
        "y": 400
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "I only held the rope",
        "description": "Hobb is talking, or starting to. He begins where it hurts, with the rope, slowly and in pieces, wiping his face with his good hand, and he won't say more where the whole Rat can hear: the player has to find him somewhere quieter (the scullery, the landing outside, the corner with Nan keeping the room off him) or give him a reason. The rest comes as the player asks for it, with no dice, one thing per reply and in his own words: his master's name (asked straight, in a whisper), the Heron and tonight's loading, how a body gets in (his token: it's only brass, and he gives it up if asked), the girl paid a crown at first light. He knows only his own part of the night, so he doesn't brief anyone on the Heron. Answer what they actually ask; never put questions in their mouth or run the interview for them. If a trick worked and he thinks he's talking to his master's people, he tells it as proof he has kept faith: the same truth. Kell writes it all down. When it's out, he asks what's to become of him: he's terrified of the noose and more terrified of his master, and he has to be at the river door at the eleventh bell or his master will know something is wrong (if the bell has rung already, he has minutes, not an hour). The beat ends on the player's choice: Kell takes him in, he goes to work (as their man inside, or keeping his head down), or he runs. Don't decide it for them.",
        "cast": {
          "hobb": "Tells the loft first ('I only held the rope'), then answers what he's asked, one thing at a time; asked straight for the Master's name, gives it in a whisper with a look at the door. When it's out, asks what's to become of him. Gives up the token if asked.",
          "kell": "Writes it down where he can watch her do it. Says once, plainly, that she has enough to take him in now; whether she does it tonight is the investigator's call, and if she does, it's by the book outside Nan's door.",
          "nan-quill": "Keeps the room off his back with a look and a rule number, and keeps her ears open.",
          "dilly": "Sits with him. Glares at anyone who frightened it out of him.",
          "arlo": "If he hears what the hand was, he's horrified and loud about it, until Nan gives him a look.",
          "cob": "If he's near enough to hear, listens from behind something; if Hobb says there was nobody else in the loft, his face says otherwise."
        },
        "pressure": "Hobb keeps looking at the door: every exchange without an answer to what's to become of him brings him nearer standing up, and at the eleventh bell he must go.",
        "establishes": [
          "hobb-talked"
        ],
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "hobb-bolts",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 860,
        "y": 680
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "Out of his chair",
        "description": "Hobb is up and bolting in a panic, because someone pushed too hard or too clumsily: out of his chair like a wall falling over, across the room and out of the door onto the wet stairs, up towards the city (frightened men run to their masters). This is a short, sharp moment: the door, the wet stairs and the dark are a few strides away. The player can let him go, go after him (a tackle on the stairs, a grab at his coat, a sprint to cut him off), or call something after him that might stop him. Indoors, rule three holds; out on the stairs, it doesn't, and Kell will back a lawful arrest if there's evidence for one. Caught, he doesn't fight: he sits on the wet steps like a felled tree, and it starts coming out of him, the loft first. If he gets away, he's gone up into the city to warn his master; whatever he owned to before he ran is in Kell's notebook, and what the room knows (where he works) can still be had. If he's talked back to his chair without telling it, he sits by the door with his coat on, and goes at the bell.",
        "cast": {
          "hobb": "Wants out: up the stairs into the dark. Caught, he doesn't fight: he sits down on the wet steps, and it comes out.",
          "kell": "If the investigator goes after him, she goes too; if he's down on the steps, she stands where he can see her writing.",
          "nan-quill": "Nobody fights in her house. Outside is the river's business.",
          "arlo": "On his feet, shouting after the champion, half jeering and half frightened.",
          "dilly": "On her feet with the cards spilled: 'Hobb! My lamb!'",
          "cob": "Out of the scullery the moment the big man's gone, white as a sheet."
        },
        "pressure": "Hobb is through the door and onto the wet stairs, and in a moment he'll be gone into the dark.",
        "establishes": [
          "hobb-bolted"
        ],
        "lore": [
          "Places/River Stairs.md"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "eleventh-bell",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 860,
        "y": 960
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "The eleventh bell",
        "description": "The eleventh bell rings across the water, and the night shift goes down the stairs. Hobb gets up, pulls his hood on, lights his lantern and goes to his night's work, as he must: if he isn't at the river door when his master comes, his master will know something is wrong. If anyone tries to stop him he says why, once, before he goes. This is a short moment: the door and the dark stairs down to the wharves are a few strides away. The player can let him go, follow him, stop him with one last appeal, or have Kell take him in if there's evidence; indoors, rule three holds. If he's already talking, he still has to go, and asks once more what's to become of him.",
        "cast": {
          "hobb": "On his feet, hood up, lantern lit. If anyone tries to stop him, he says why he must, once: if he's not at the Heron's river door when the Master comes, the Master will know, and the Master knows where his mum lives.",
          "kell": "Says once, quietly, whether she has enough to take him in, and waits for the investigator's word.",
          "nan-quill": "Lets him go: nobody's stopped at her door. Outside is the river's business.",
          "dilly": "'Go careful, my lamb.' She'd keep him if she could.",
          "arlo": "Quiet, for once, watching the champion go.",
          "cob": "Out of the scullery once the big man's at the door, watching him go with the tray held like a shield."
        },
        "pressure": "Hobb is out of the door and going down the stairs towards the wharves, his lantern swinging; in a moment it's round the bend and gone.",
        "lore": [
          "Places/River Stairs.md"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "hobb-talks",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 1300,
        "y": 0
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "exit",
        "title": "What Big Hobb knows",
        "description": "Big Hobb has told the player what he knows, or the heart of it: his part in the loft (he held the clerk's arms while his master killed him, then hauled him up on the rope), who his master is (Master Sallow of the Royal Purveyor's office) and where he works (the Heron, loading tonight), and as much of the rest as they asked for (how to get in at night, his token). It's in Kell's notebook as he said it. Hobb isn't under arrest and hasn't run: he's going to his night's work at the Heron, as their man inside if he agreed to be, or keeping his head down if not. The player leaves the Rat with it.",
        "closing": "Close on the player leaving the Rat with what they came for: Hobb's face as they go (or his broad back going down the stairs ahead of them at the bell, his lantern swinging), Nan's look, the river below. Kell closes her notebook on it.",
        "result": "good",
        "exitKind": "progress",
        "next": "wharf-night",
        "effects": [
          {
            "op": "remove",
            "what": "hobb",
            "text": "heron-warehouse"
          },
          {
            "op": "remember",
            "who": "hobb",
            "text": "He told the King's investigator what happened in the tally loft, and who his master is.",
            "if": {
              "established": "master-named"
            }
          },
          {
            "op": "remember",
            "who": "hobb",
            "text": "He told the King's investigator what he did in the tally loft. He didn't say his master's name.",
            "unless": {
              "established": "master-named"
            }
          },
          {
            "op": "promise",
            "by": "hobb",
            "to": "$player",
            "text": "Open the Heron's river door to the player tonight and keep the gang off them.",
            "if": {
              "established": "hobb-inside"
            }
          },
          {
            "op": "flag",
            "name": "hobb_identified",
            "value": "true"
          },
          {
            "op": "flag",
            "name": "heron_known",
            "value": "true"
          },
          {
            "op": "quest",
            "quest": "heron-ledger",
            "do": "start",
            "title": "The Heron Ledger",
            "text": "Big Hobb Mallory works nights for a gentleman at the Heron warehouse, at the downriver end of the wharves. If the King's 'lost' grain goes anywhere, it goes through there."
          },
          {
            "op": "quest",
            "quest": "purveyors-man",
            "do": "advance",
            "stage": "hobb_talked",
            "text": "Big Hobb Mallory says Master Sallow killed Prout in the tally loft while Hobb held his arms, and had Hobb hoist him; the stolen grain goes through the Heron warehouse.",
            "if": {
              "established": "master-named"
            }
          },
          {
            "op": "quest",
            "quest": "purveyors-man",
            "do": "advance",
            "stage": "hobb_talked",
            "text": "Big Hobb Mallory says his master killed Prout in the tally loft while Hobb held his arms, and had Hobb hoist him. His master is Master Sallow of the Royal Purveyor's office, and the stolen grain goes through the Heron warehouse.",
            "unless": {
              "established": "master-named"
            }
          },
          {
            "op": "journal",
            "text": "Big Hobb talked: he held Prout in the loft while Master Sallow killed him. The grain goes through the Heron warehouse, and tonight a barge is loading there.",
            "if": {
              "established": "master-named"
            }
          },
          {
            "op": "journal",
            "text": "Big Hobb owned to the loft: he held Prout while his master killed him, and hauled the rope. His master is Master Sallow. The grain goes through the Heron warehouse, and tonight a barge is loading there.",
            "unless": {
              "established": "master-named"
            }
          },
          {
            "op": "journal",
            "text": "Hobb will open the Heron's river door to us tonight, for as long as his nerve holds.",
            "if": {
              "established": "hobb-inside"
            }
          }
        ],
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "taken-in",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 1300,
        "y": 220
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "exit",
        "title": "Taken in",
        "description": "Sergeant Kell has arrested Big Hobb on evidence she accepts (his own admission, or a witness with the wound that matches set beside it) and is taking him up the river stairs to the watch-house cells. Whatever he told the player before he was taken, they have, and whatever he owned to is in Kell's notebook as he said it; where the grain goes they know, and who his master is they know or will by morning (in the cells he tells all of it, slowly, to whoever writes it down). He won't be at the Heron tonight, and his master will find his porter missing at the bell.",
        "closing": "Close on the arrest done by the book outside Nan's door: Kell's proper words, Hobb's great shoulders going down, the Rat silent behind them, the river below. Kell will see him locked up and come on to wherever the investigator is going next.",
        "result": "good",
        "exitKind": "progress",
        "next": "wharf-night",
        "effects": [
          {
            "op": "remove",
            "what": "hobb",
            "text": "watch-house"
          },
          {
            "op": "remember",
            "who": "hobb",
            "text": "Sergeant Kell arrested him outside the Drowned Rat for the clerk's death, with the King's investigator there."
          },
          {
            "op": "remember",
            "who": "kell",
            "text": "She arrested Big Hobb Mallory for Prout's murder outside the Drowned Rat and took him to the watch-house cells."
          },
          {
            "op": "flag",
            "name": "hobb_identified",
            "value": "true"
          },
          {
            "op": "flag",
            "name": "heron_known",
            "value": "true"
          },
          {
            "op": "quest",
            "quest": "heron-ledger",
            "do": "start",
            "title": "The Heron Ledger",
            "text": "Big Hobb Mallory works nights for a gentleman at the Heron warehouse, at the downriver end of the wharves. If the King's 'lost' grain goes anywhere, it goes through there."
          },
          {
            "op": "quest",
            "quest": "purveyors-man",
            "do": "advance",
            "stage": "hobb_arrested",
            "text": "Big Hobb Mallory, the big man with the bandaged hand, is in the watch-house cells. His master is Master Sallow of the Royal Purveyor's office.",
            "if": {
              "established": "master-known"
            }
          },
          {
            "op": "quest",
            "quest": "purveyors-man",
            "do": "advance",
            "stage": "hobb_arrested",
            "text": "Big Hobb Mallory, the big man with the bandaged hand, is in the watch-house cells, and what he knows of his master will come out there.",
            "unless": {
              "established": "master-known"
            }
          },
          {
            "op": "journal",
            "text": "Kell took Big Hobb in. He's in the watch-house cells tonight, and won't be at the Heron when his master comes."
          }
        ],
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gone-to-work",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 1300,
        "y": 440
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "exit",
        "title": "Hobb goes to work",
        "description": "Hobb has gone, or goes at the bell, to his night's work at the Heron without having told the player the heart of what he knows (his part in the loft, who his master is, where he works), or anything at all, and the player has left the Rat too: on his heels to follow him down to the wharves, or ahead of him, knowing where he's going, to wait in the dark.",
        "closing": "Close on Hobb's broad back going down the river stairs towards the wharves, his lantern swinging, and the player at the top of the steps in the dark (or, if they went ahead, the wharves below and his lantern coming down).",
        "result": "mixed",
        "exitKind": "leave",
        "next": "wharf-night",
        "effects": [
